  "notes": "The Cheiron ballad unit — the UK/Irish boy-band and talent-show specialists. The research pairs 73–96 with a half-time feel and the backbeat on 3, so `halfTime` is true and the snare is `halftime_3`; the 105–130 uptempo band is the straight-time wing and is named here rather than encoded. Four flat-side major tonics are given (A-flat, B-flat, C, F) so `keys` is authored, over I–V–vi–IV and vi–IV–I–V. Their trademark is a modulation and, crucially, how it is announced: \"the **final-chorus semitone/whole-tone modulation**… is almost always announced by a whole-bar tom-and-crash fill\". A key change is outside the generator's reach, but the announcement is not — the tom and crash lanes lead the percs and the big-fill schedule is set to 16 bars so the whole-bar figure reads as an event. Three more drum facts are quoted: \"kick 1 and 3\", \"8th shaker\", and \"tambourine from chorus 2\", with the whole kit \"low in the mix\", which is the soft velocity tier. \"A full drum drop-out for the bridge's last two bars\" is `dropOutBeats` at 0.9 over 8 beats. The melodies are external toplines \"voiced for group unison in the verse, harmony in the chorus\", and \"note density very low\" — 2–5 with a legato articulation and a low counter ratio. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium and says the drum specifics are inferred from the records. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability and `bpm.mode` 84. ⚠ **`bassline.rhythm` is `boom_chick`, not `reese_sustain`** — the research names the figure outright: *“synth bass or played bass on roots, whole/half notes in verses moving to 8ths from chorus 2; the piano's left hand doubles it”*. A held `reese_sustain` writes one note per chord and says the opposite of what the entry describes.",
